@1 = ~Watch it yourself, you little squirt. Adults are walking! Not that your feeble mind could ever comprehend the intellect and importance of the personage standing right next to you.~
@2 = ~Pah. Someone whose robes I can use to wipe my behind, no doubt.~
@3 = ~You insolent... no, Edwin Odesseiron knows better than to waste breath on a simple-minded peasant. (Though if there were no witnesses, I'd blast the little cretin across Faerun.)~
@4 = ~Take care, Edwin. There's no telling when the little fellow might turn his wit on you again.~
@5 = ~Urgh... Flaming Fist. Nothing but a bunch of degenerates and simpletons. They might've blinded all the foolish commoners with that optimistic law-enforcement notion, but I know better how false they are.~
@6 = ~What was that?~
@7 = ~I said we will absolutely abide by your holy law. Haven't you heard me clearly enough?~
@8 = ~I don't like sarcasm.~
@9 = ~Then a walk through Baldur's Gate must be terribly trying for you, officer.~
@10 = ~Nor did I choose my orcish heritage, and yet people gawk at my face like I am the ugliest object at a circus display.~
@11 = ~Then you know something of what I've endured. Perhaps we have more in common than either of us realised.~
@12 = ~Well, I for one have seen much worse faces in a tavern on a morning after. Pay them no mind.~
@13 = ~Tiax agrees. Once Tiax rules, fish will no longer soil the waters with their stench.~
@14 = ~Rules? What are you, voting for a Grand Duke or something?~
@15 = ~Such insolence is despicable!~
@16 = ~Even a filthy hobgoblin makes fun of your inflated, gormless head. Isn't that a wake-up call for you?~
@17 = ~Cyric's word is ultimate and final. Tiax is about to become a ruler, and unintelligent people throwing insults will be tortured eternally in his Pit of Despair.~
@18 = ~Seek help.~
@19 = ~A low degree of intellect is to be expected in a city like this. Silly people applies to anyone. Anyone but me, that is.~
@20 = ~My ears are about to bleed if I hear one more boast from this idiotic gnome.~
@21 = ~I have rescued a fair maiden or two from deep water. It's the gratitude that makes getting soaked worthwhile.~
@22 = ~Aye, and next time they can thank me by staying on the pier.~
@23 = ~He's right to warn us. Umberlee is an evil goddess, and tinkering with her or her worshippers brings nothing but trouble.~
@24 = ~Then perhaps we'll keep to the docks. I'd rather have solid ground beneath my boots in any case.~
@25 = ~Can you blame them? You are sooooo cuddly.~
@26 = ~No. I am soooo out of here.~
@27 = ~Worry not, my little friend. Minsc and Boo carry weapons to kick evil in their... evil butts. We would never harm an innocent like you. Right, Boo?~
@28 = ~Minsc. I prithee, keep this rodent confined within thy pouch. Thou makest thyself appear utterly ridiculous, and furthermore, thou bringest shame upon us too.~
@29 = ~I wish I could offer you more than warm words of encouragement, but we live in difficult times. In Tempus's name we are trying to do something about it, but it's a long process. You may need to brace yourself and be patient.~
@30 = ~Life is so unfair.~
@31 = ~The world is a complicated place, and in order to survive, you need to adapt. I promise we will put in our best efforts to end this city's misery.~
@32 = ~Next time don't. Opinions are fragile... my sword is sharp. It cuts through the fragile like a razor through skin.~
@33 = ~An opinion costs nothing, friend. Let him keep his and save the sword for an enemy who deserves it.~
@34 = ~Do not call me your friend, elf. And save your advices for someone who cares.~
@35 = ~As little affection as I have for elves, I can't help but agree.~
@36 = ~A rare moment of agreement between our peoples. Perhaps I should mark the date.~
@37 = ~Do not become accustomed to it.~
@38 = ~Poor child. If only she were old enough to comprehend the hardships of life with more maturity and understanding.~
@39 = ~Such a t-tragedy to happen to one so young. My heart aches for her.~
@40 = ~Death isn't something to be mourned. Accept it and move on. Although, it appears she accepted it... in a way.~
@41 = ~The youngster made a halfling tear up. I wish I could cheer her up, but there isn't anything to cheer up. She takes it very well due to how little she understands.~
@42 = ~Boo says you will be reunited with your dad for certain when the time comes. You just need to be brave.~
@43 = ~I know, sir. Thank you.~
@44 = ~Your father would be proud of such a brave girl. Hold fast to your dreams, little one.~
@45 = ~Have you seen yourself, jaluk? Rags muddied and face worn out. I would've thought you were among local beggars.~
@46 = ~This is called hard work! You should try it sometime, drow! Besides, what would you know? Your people represent the most vile evil.~
@47 = ~Agreeable argument.~
@48 = ~My people believe in discipline, hierarchy and reprimand. Our traditions may be... unapologetic, but at least we are true to ourselves. Even though I don't fully agree with some of them.~
@49 = ~It matters not. I don't need to hear about your chastising rules, lethal punishments or warped ideas of law and order. I will go back to... my corner. Find your own.~
@50 = ~Your corner? So you are a street urchin after all.~
@51 = ~Shut up.~
@52 = ~Helm does not measure the worth of a watchman by the length of his shadow, nor by the scales upon his skin. He measures it by the steadfastness of his spirit and the loyalty in his heart.~
@53 = ~The duties of a priest of the Watching Eye are heavy. Your boy will have to learn the absolute meaning of vigilance, protection and sacrifice. He will have to stand as a shield for those who cannot defend themselves, even when the night is cold and the watch seems endless.~
@54 = ~But seeing the discipline you are trying to teach him, and the peace you have built for your family here in the city, I have no doubt he carries the right foundation. It takes a strong soul to guard a home, and an even stronger one to desire that duty for the whole world.~
@55 = ~I thank you kindly, dear paladin. It means a lot to hear it from a priest of Helm. I shall pass your words to my son. I'm sure it will give him more hope.~
@56 = ~Fine work, paladin. Clangeddin knows I ain't got no love for kobolds, but even they deserve a bit o' hope if they're proven to be loyal and sturdy like good stone.~
@57 = ~A sound education will take your son further than most swords ever could. I wish him good fortune.~
@58 = ~You're a sweetheart after my own heart. Why can't everyone be friends, spread friendship and joy, and be nice to one another? I adore your worldview, because it matches mine perfectly.~
@59 = ~I am pleased to meet you. We halflings should stick together, although you found your crowd, I see. I will remember you fondly.~
@60 = ~I agree wholeheartedly with that point of view. I always admired halflings for their spirit and ability to look past another person's flaws. They treat everyone so kindly.~
@61 = ~Like Alora does with Edwin.~
@62 = ~Wh... Imoen! I... be quiet, child! Alora and I are nothing but... acquaintances.~
@63 = ~Cheer up, grumpy. We are friends. Good friends.~
@64 = ~Y... yeah. Of course. (His voice turns to a whisper.) Alora, please. Not in front of everyone.~
@65 = ~Don't be so shy. Friends do not judge, and Imoen is a friend of ours, right?~
@66 = ~(She giggles.) Sure thing.~
@67 = ~Madame Skie. Pardon my intrusion on your personal space, but... who are these people? Does your father know you're with them?~
@68 = ~Do not worry, Desmund. They are my friends. We met at a... tavern. They are interested in art and piano, just like me. I assure you. B-but... Desmund?~
@69 = ~Yes?~
@70 = ~Don't tell daddy about this.~
@71 = ~Because he wouldn't like it?~
@72 = ~No. Because he has enough head-scratchers in his life to attend to, and I don't need him... ahem, worrying about me. I'll be back before he manages to recite his every-morning lecture about how I'm supposed to speak with courtesy and study boring books he supplies.~
@73 = ~But...~
@74 = ~Bye-bye.~
@75 = ~Don't worry, Harvey. I know how much you love your roses. I'll make sure not to repeat the incident with the outdoor latrine.~
@76 = ~Outdoor... latrine?~
@77 = ~Oh, it's nothing. I'm a lady with needs and ladies need to let go of... etiquette, and go wild sometimes. Enjoy life.~
@78 = ~I know a little something about that. I was imprisoned in that mine you mentioned. Your kin did not treat me gently. Not that I'm prejudiced, just saying.~
@79 = ~Sorry to hear. I hold absolutely no liking toward my kin from the Nashkel mines. They asked for it. On the other hand, they were what I call strays: kobolds that were born uppity, in poverty and misery.~
@80 = ~And that is meant to justify their actions?~
@81 = ~That does not justify anything. Anything at all, but it sheds some light on their circumstances. Lowlife kobolds with possibly no home, no future and little capacity for morals. I am truly sorry you experienced this.~
@82 = ~Thank you. If only I'd met more nuanced kobolds like you instead back then.~
@83 = ~Culture is for lifeless sages. Who with common sense would ever put culture over money?~
@84 = ~There are treasures money can't buy, Kagain. Though I suspect you'd find a way to put a price on them.~
@85 = ~If it's worth havin', someone'll pay for it.~
@86 = ~Five gold for a beer? Why, I'd sooner buy the whole cask and invite the street to share it!~
@87 = ~A love of books is uncommon enough among adventurers, let alone kobolds. I'd be glad to hear one of your stories someday.~
@88 = ~Shimmerweave is lovely, but beauty need not empty one's purse. Even a plain ribbon can turn a head when worn with confidence.~
@89 = ~A rather forward man, by the sound of it. Some people have no sense of when their attention is unwelcome.~
@90 = ~Beholders. Creatures as rare as they are dangerous. The sight of one's severed head while drinking an ale would be decidedly unpleasant. I am not surprised she removed it.~
@91 = ~I understand the s-s-sentiment. Beholders do not exactly inspire... p-peace of mind.~
@92 = ~Sounds scary. Those are the one-eyed cyclops thingies, aren't they?~
@93 = ~Actually, Imoen, they aren't cyclopes. Not even remotely related. Beholders are alien aberrations from realms beyond our own. Their single great eye is about the only thing they have in common with a cyclops.~
@94 = ~I don't care where they come from. They send shivers down my spine! Winthrop once made me a teddy bear that looked like one of them. He used to frighten me with it when I was little and refused to clean up after myself. I think he rather enjoyed punishing me that way, too.~
@95 = ~Beholder body parts are a gold mine. Any alchemist, mage or collector would pay good money for one of their eyes. Provided we cut it out carefully enough, mind you. Nobody will pay a copper for damaged goods.~
@96 = ~(Sigh.) I sincerely hope you aren't entertaining the notion of venturing into some dreadful cavern or forgotten catacomb to hunt one down after hearing all this.~
@97 = ~He doesn't seem particularly determined to do his job. I wager we could walk closer to the door... no, even straight through it, and he wouldn't lift a finger to stop us. Males cannot be trusted with such responsibilities, after all.~
@98 = ~In plain words, "Go on in, and I won't lift a finger to stop you." Typical.~
